MAKERCFALSE -- what happens to $? and $VARS through 'wsl -- bash -c'
Measured 2026-08-29 on this host.  Every line below is a real run, not a transcription.
Git-bash -> wsl.exe -> Gentoo.  Each block states WANT so a reader can score it.

== A. LOCAL isolation, no WSL at all -- establishes the ordinary shell behaviour ==
   bare              : 1
   bash -c SINGLE    : 1
   bash -c DOUBLE    : 0
   WANT 1 / 1 / 0.  The double-quoted form is the outer shell baking in a LITERAL.

== B. THROUGH WSL -- and this is the part that is NOT ordinary shell behaviour ==
   -c single, $? : 0
   -c single, $X : []
   WANT 1 and [hello].  Got 0 and [] -- SINGLE QUOTES DO NOT PROTECT through this path.

== C. What DOES survive, so the diagnosis is not 'wsl mangles everything' ==
   quoted 2 spaces: a  b$
   Both spaces survive.  So it is not a naive re-split of the command string.

== D. THE TWO WORKING FORMS, both measured ==
   backslash-escaped \$?  : 1
   backslash-escaped \$X  : [hello]
   script FILE           : FILE_GOT=hello FILE_RC=1 
   WANT 1 / [hello] / FILE_GOT=hello FILE_RC=1.  Both forms are sound.

MAKERC_PROBE_DONE

== E. IS build_asan.sh ACTUALLY DEFECTIVE?  The row's witness says yes.  Measured: NO. ==
   It is invoked as a FILE, never through 'bash -c', so section B does not reach it.
   :32  rm -rf "$dir"; mkdir -p "$dir"   <- FRESHLY WIPED every run
   :41  make -j12 > build.log 2>&1
   :42  echo "  make rc=$?  objs=$(find src -name '*.o' | wc -l)"
   :43  if [ -x gxemul ]; then ... else BUILD FAILED; return 1

   Two things the row assumed and neither holds:
   (1) '$? inside an echo' is NOT clobbered by the $() later in the same word.
       $? BEFORE $() : rc=1 objs=7   (want rc=1 -- build_asan.sh shape, SOUND)
       measured in one word : rc=1  objs=7   <- want rc=1
       the TRAP for contrast: objs=7  rc=0   <- want rc=0
   (2) the existence check is a SOUND proxy here BECAUSE of the rm -rf at :32 --
       a stale binary from an earlier run cannot survive into the decision.

   HONEST RESIDUAL: :42's rc is INFORMATIONAL -- nothing asserts on it.  That is a
   real weakness in the abstract, and it is not the reported defect: with the dir
   wiped first, a failed make leaves no gxemul and :46 reports BUILD FAILED, rc 1.

MAKERC_SECTION_E_DONE

== F. THE DEFECT BIT ITS OWN DIAGNOSTICIAN, ~1 HOUR AFTER SECTION B WAS WRITTEN. ==
   Checking for .MUTANT sentinels, I typed the BROKEN form out of habit:
       wsl -- bash -c 'for f in /tmp/gx*/.MUTANT; do echo "--- $f"; ... done'
   Every $f and $d came back EMPTY, so the loop ran four times over blank paths and
   printed four lines of '(no binary)'.

   *** THE PHANTOM WAS REASSURING, WHICH IS THE WORST DIRECTION. ***  It said the
   trees hold no binaries and so cannot be measured by mistake.  Re-run with the
   escaped form, ALL FOUR HOLD BINARIES, 6 MB to 107 MB, one to four days old.
   A wrong answer that alarms gets checked; a wrong answer that reassures gets filed.

   This is the carrier's own finding about itself: knowing a rule and having it fire
   on the right object are different problems.  The rule was measured, written into
   the ledger and into this file, and then not applied by its author within the hour.

MAKERC_SECTION_F_DONE

== G. A SIBLING TRAP, SAME FAMILY, FOUND 2026-08-29: git-bash `strings` RETURNS ZERO ==
   ON A LINUX ELF, SILENTLY.

   Checking whether build/gxemul carried the #490 fix:

       strings build/gxemul | wc -l                    -> 0        (git-bash)
       wsl -- strings /mnt/c/.../build/gxemul | wc -l  -> 44054    (WSL)

   The binary is 6.1 MB, ELF 64-bit, NOT STRIPPED.  Zero strings is impossible.

   *** AND THE WRONG ANSWER WAS THE ALARMING KIND, WHICH IS WHY IT WAS CAUGHT. ***
   It said the binary contained NO decline strings while its SOURCE carried the fix and
   its mtime was NEWER than the source -- i.e. it manufactured a textbook stale-binary
   finding, the exact class this project has paid for twice.  I was one step from
   recording it.

   The control settled it in one command: the same probe on a known-post-fix clone,
   through WSL, returned 14 `declined` and 24 `dreamcast_gdrom`.  Re-run against build/
   through WSL: both #490 messages present, binary sound.

   THE RULE, and it is the denominator rule again: **`0 of 0` is not a measurement.**
   A tool that reports zero TOTAL on a 6 MB binary has failed, not answered.  Always
   print the denominator -- `wc -l` on the whole extraction -- beside any grep of it.

   Note this one differs from the dollar-stripping above in a way that matters: that trap
   makes a wrong answer that REASSURES, this one makes a wrong answer that ALARMS.  The
   alarming kind gets checked; the reassuring kind gets filed.

MAKERC_SECTION_G_DONE
